USER USER   1. Declare the new common module and import NixCryptoArgs and Error from it.
USER USER   2. Add three new CLI flags to the USAGE string and Args struct:
USER USER        --openssl-symmetric-key-id
USER USER        --openssl-symmetric-key-derivation
USER USER        --openssl-symmetric-key-iterations
USER USER   3. Implement to_nix_crypto_args on Args, which calls NixCryptoArgs::from_flags
USER USER      with the sled store, log file, and log level flags.
USER USER   4. Move the subcommand dispatch logic into an Args::main method, which returns
USER USER      Result<(), Error> and is called from the top-level main function.
USER USER   5. Pass the three new symmetric key flags through to export::resolve_args.
USER USER 
USER USER 
USER USER 6. DATA FLOW SUMMARY
USER USER ---------------------
USER USER 
USER USER 6.1 Export Decryptable (Nix primop path)
USER USER 
USER USER   Nix code calls:
USER USER     private-key.export-decryptable-pkey { key-id = ...; key-derivation = ...;
USER USER                                            iterations = ...; }
USER USER 
USER USER   This calls the Nix primop:
USER USER     openssl.export-decryptable-pkey symmetric-key-params key-ref
USER USER 
USER USER   The primop (arity 2) is handled by primop_openssl_export_decryptable_pkey in
USER USER   openssl_primops.cc, which:
USER USER     1. Reads the symmetric key identity from args[0] via
USER USER        openssl_get_symmetric_key_identity.
USER USER     2. Reads the private key identity from args[1] via
USER USER        openssl_get_private_key_identity.
USER USER     3. Calls primops->opensslExportDecryptableOpensslPkey.
USER USER 
USER USER   CryptoNixPrimops::opensslExportDecryptableOpensslPkey delegates to:
USER USER     cryptoNix()->cxx_export_decryptable_openssl_pkey(symmetric_key, credential)
USER USER 
USER USER   CxxNixCrypto::cxx_export_decryptable_openssl_pkey calls:
USER USER     export_decryptable(&self.0, &symmetric_key,
USER USER                        &OpensslPrivateKeyIdentityWrapper(&credential))
USER USER 
USER USER   decryptable::export_decryptable:
USER USER     1. get_or_derive(SymmetricKeyIdentity(key)) -> SymmetricKeyValue
USER USER     2. get_or_derive(OpensslPrivateKeyIdentityWrapper(credential)) -> Key
USER USER     3. get_or_derive(EncryptionParamsKey(sym_id, cred)) -> EncryptionParams
USER USER     4. credential_value.export() -> Vec<u8> (PEM bytes of private key)
USER USER     5. encrypt_to_pem(sym_key_value, salt, iv, plaintext) -> PEM String
USER USER 
USER USER   The resulting PEM string is returned up the call stack and set as a Nix
USER USER   string value.
USER USER 
USER USER 6.2 Symmetric Key Passphrase Export (CLI path)
USER USER 
USER USER   User invokes:
USER USER     nix-crypto-service export secret \
USER USER       --identity-type openssl-symmetric-key \
USER USER       --openssl-symmetric-key-id <id> \
USER USER       --openssl-symmetric-key-derivation pbkdf2 \
USER USER       --openssl-symmetric-key-iterations 600000 \
USER USER       --output-file <path>
USER USER 
USER USER   Args::main calls export::resolve_args, which calls resolve_identity_type,
USER USER   producing IdentityType::OpensslSymmetricKey { key_id, key_derivation,
USER USER   iterations }.
USER USER 
USER USER   export::run_secret:
USER USER     1. Calls args.nix_crypto_args.init() to construct a CryptoNix instance.
USER USER     2. Constructs OpensslSymmetricKeyIdentity { key_id, key_derivation,
USER USER        iterations }.
USER USER     3. Calls crypto_nix.openssl_symmetric_key_passphrase(&identity).
USER USER     4. Delegates to decryptable::get_symmetric_key_passphrase, which calls
USER USER        get_or_derive(SymmetricKeyIdentity(key)) and returns
USER USER        symmetric_key_value.random_secret.
USER USER     5. Writes the passphrase string to the output file.
USER USER 
USER USER 
USER USER 7. SECURITY NOTES
USER USER ------------------
USER USER 
USER USER   - The random_secret is generated using OpenSSL's rand_bytes, which is a
USER USER     cryptographically secure random number generator.
USER USER   - The random_secret is 16 bytes (128 bits) of entropy, base64-encoded to a
USER USER     24-character string.
USER USER   - The AES key is derived from random_secret using PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with a
USER USER     configurable iteration count. The default is 600,000 iterations, which is
USER USER     consistent with current NIST recommendations for PBKDF2 with SHA-256.
USER USER   - A unique salt and IV are generated for each (symmetric_key, credential)
USER USER     pair and persisted in the store. This ensures that the same plaintext
USER USER     encrypted with the same key always produces the same ciphertext (required
USER USER     for Nix referential transparency), while preventing IV reuse across
USER USER     different (key, credential) pairs.
USER USER   - The passphrase (random_secret) written to disk by nix-crypto-service is
USER USER     sensitive. The caller is responsible for ensuring the output file has
USER USER     appropriate permissions.
USER USER   - Private keys are never exposed in the Nix language. The export-decryptable-pkey
USER USER     primop returns an encrypted PEM string, not the raw private key.
USER USER 
USER USER 
